---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout
  title="Our Story | Why We Make Yoghurt of Youth"
  description="The story behind Yoghurt of Youth — why a science-minded founder set out to make fresh, live, strain-specific L. reuteri yoghurt that didn't exist anywhere in the UK."
>
  <section
    id="story"
    class="relative scroll-mt-32 md:scroll-mt-24 bg-white text-slate-800 py-12"
  >
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-8">
      <h1 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-bold text-slate-900">
        Our Story
      </h1>

      <p class="mt-4 text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
        <strong>Yoghurt of Youth</strong> started for a simple reason: I wanted this yoghurt to
        exist, and it didn't. Not in the UK, not anywhere I could find. So I
        learned to make it myself, and then decided other people should be able to
        have it too.
      </p>

      <div class="mt-8 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50 border border-slate-200 px-6 py-5">
        <p class="text-md uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-slate-900 font-semibold">What we're doing</p>
        <p class="mt-3 text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
          <strong class="text-slate-900">Our mission</strong> is to make
          <em>L. reuteri</em> yoghurt we couldn't find anywhere.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
          <strong class="text-slate-900">Our standard</strong> is to be the most rigorous
          maker of it in the UK, with defined strains, measured fermentation, and genuine science.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
          <strong class="text-slate-900">Our vision</strong> is a future where gut health is
          built on single strain ferments rather than pills or undefined mixed cultures.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2 class="mt-8 text-xl font-semibold text-slate-900">A science background, and a rabbit hole</h2>
      <p class="mt-2 text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
        My background is in physics. That training leaves you with a particular
        habit: when something interests you, you go to the source, read the actual
        research, and try to understand the mechanism rather than the marketing.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-2 text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
        A few years ago, circumstances led me deep into the science of the gut
        microbiome, the vast community of bacteria that lives in the gut and
        behaves like an organ in its own right. The more I read, the more struck I
        was by how foundational it appears to be, and how much genuine, published
        research there is behind specific bacterial strains, in particular the
        strains of <em>Lactobacillus reuteri</em> our yoghurts are built around.
      </p>

      <h2 class="mt-8 text-xl font-semibold text-slate-900">The thing that didn't exist</h2>
      <p class="mt-2 text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
        Here is what I found: if you wanted a fresh, live yoghurt made with
        specific, well-researched <em>L. reuteri</em> strains, at a genuinely high
        live-culture count, your options were essentially none. You could buy dried
        capsules sitting on a shelf. You could buy generic supermarket yoghurts
        with undefined cultures. Or, if you wanted the real thing, you could do
        what I ended up doing: spend time learning to efficiently ferment it yourself.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-2 text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
        That struck me as a gap worth closing. The interest in gut health is huge
        and growing, the research on these strains is real and public, and yet the
        product itself simply wasn't available to anyone who didn't want to become
        an amateur fermenter first.
      </p>

      <h2 class="mt-8 text-xl font-semibold text-slate-900">Made properly</h2>
      <p class="mt-2 text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
        So that is what Yoghurt of Youth is: the yoghurt I wished I could buy. Live
        and fresh, fermented in small batches with specific researched strains,
        chilled and delivered within days rather than dried into a capsule for a
        shelf. Lactose-free, with no added sweeteners. Made with the same
        attention to the science that sent me down this path in the first place.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-2 text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
        Just as important to me is how we talk about it. The wellness world is full
        of overclaiming, of products promising to fix everything. I would rather do
        the opposite: explain the genuine research, and clarify where the
        science is strong and where it is still early. You'll see that throughout
        this site, on our
        <a href="/theory" class="underline hover:text-amber-500 transition">theory of gut dysbiosis</a>,
        our pages on the
        <a href="/skin" class="underline hover:text-amber-500 transition">gut–skin</a>
        and <a href="/hormones" class="underline hover:text-amber-500 transition">gut–hormone</a>
        connections, and everywhere else. We tell you what the evidence shows, and
        where it doesn't yet.
      </p>

      <h2 class="mt-8 text-xl font-semibold text-slate-900">Holding the standard</h2>
      <p class="mt-2 text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
        There is no shortage of gut health products. What there is a shortage of is
        precision. Most fermented products on the shelf will tell you they contain
        live cultures without telling you which ones, in what quantity, or on what
        evidence. We think that is the wrong way round, and we have built this
        business to do the opposite.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-2 text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
        Every batch we make is fermented with a single, named, well-researched strain
        of <em>L. reuteri</em>. The fermentation is temperature-controlled and
        finished to a measured pH rather than a guess. The strains rotate on a
        defined cycle, so you know what you are receiving and why. And when we write
        about the science, we say plainly where the evidence is strong and where it
        is still early, because a claim you cannot support is worth nothing.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-2 text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
        That is what we mean by holding the standard. Not the loudest claims, but the
        most careful product and the most sincere account of it.
      </p>

      <h2 class="mt-8 text-xl font-semibold text-slate-900">Where gut health is going</h2>
      <p class="mt-2 text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
        We think the future of gut health belongs to fresh, single-strain
        fermentation. The clearest research in this field has always studied specific
        strains, and a ferment delivers them alive along with everything they produced
        while fermenting, which is something a capsule cannot do. We outline the full
        case in our <a href="/compare" class="underline hover:text-amber-500 transition">comparison of capsules, traditional ferments and our yoghurt</a>. We want to usher in that future.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-8 text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
        <strong>- Zain, Founder</strong>
      </p>

      <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
        <a href="/about" class="rounded-2xl bg-slate-900 text-white px-6 py-2.5 text-sm font-semibold hover:bg-slate-700 transition">
          Learn More
        </a>
        <a href="/shop" class="rounded-2xl bg-amber-400 text-slate-900 px-6 py-2.5 text-sm font-semibold hover:bg-amber-300 transition">
          Shop Now
        </a>
      </div>

      <p class="mt-8 text-slate-600 text-xs leading-relaxed">
        Yoghurt of Youth makes live fermented foods, not medicines. Nothing on this
        page is medical advice, or a claim that our products treat, cure or prevent
        any condition. If you have health concerns, please speak with a qualified
        healthcare professional.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>